tent\": \"91\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [236, 164, 916, 192], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Federal Agencies' Financial Reports\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [67, 221, 567, 363], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Section 114 of the Budget and Accounting Procedures Act of 1950 (31 U.S.C. 3513a) requires each executive agency to provide the Secretary of the Treasury with reports and information on the agency's financial condition and operations. Although these provisions do not apply to the Federal Government's legislative and judicial branches, they are encouraged to submit reports so that the Secretary of the Treasury can prepare comprehensive reports on all of the financial activities of the Government.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [67, 378, 568, 572], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Federal agencies submit four financial reports and three supporting reports. The financial reports include: Report on Financial Position (SF 220), Report on Operations (SF 221), Report on Cash Flow (SF 222), and Report on Reconciliation (SF 223). The supporting reports are: Direct and Guaranteed Loans Reported by Agency and Program Due from the Public (SF 220-8), Report on Accounts and Loans Receivable Due from the Public (SF 220-9), and Additional Financial Information (SF 220-1). Agencies submit SF 220-8 quarterly, and annually for publication in the Treasury Bulletin. Meanwhile, all agencies submit SF 220-9 annually, while some report quarterly on a selected basis.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [66, 589, 565, 676], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"The Treasury Financial Manual (ITFM 2-4100) sets the criteria for the submission of annual and quarterly reports in accordance with the Reporting Entities Listing (Bulletin No. 91-09). There are reports for six fund types: revolving funds, trust revolving funds, 15 major trust funds, all other trust funds, all other activity combined, and\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [586, 223, 1072, 291], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"consolidated reports of each organizational unit. Financial transactions supporting the reports are accounted for on the accrual basis, although the SF 221 can be submitted on a cash basis under certain circumstances.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [588, 310, 1072, 434], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"The Office of Management and Budget (OMB) Circular No. A-127 requires agencies to prepare reports from a budgeting and accounting system that contains an integrated data base, which is part of the agency's integrated financial management system. Reports are supposed to include all assets, liabilities, and equities relating to all programs and activities under control of the reporting agency. (Treasury reports assets of disbursing officers.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [585, 451, 1072, 536], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Reports should also include transfer appropriation accounts from other agencies, foreign currencies, operations conducted in the territories or overseas, and any monetary assets or property received, spent, or otherwise accounted for by the reporting agency. Amounts are reported to the dollar.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [588, 555, 1073, 678], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Information from SF 220-8 is presented in Table FA-1, which reflects the direct and guaranteed loans to the public through the Federal Credit Program to support credit activities. Credit program levels are controlled by authorizing legislation and appropriation acts. SF 220-8 also provides the Federal Reserve Board information to monitor the flow of funds. The accompanying chart depicts direct and guaranteed loans for the fourth quarter of fiscal 1991.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [57, 47, 95, 83], \"page_id\": 102}], \"content\": \"92\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [317, 73, 749, 101], \"page_id\": 102}], \"content\": \"FEDERAL AGENCIES FINANCIAL REPORTS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [210, 169, 847, 205], \"page_id\": 102}], \"content\": \"TABLE FA-1.--Direct and Guaranteed Loans, Sept. 30, 1991\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [284, 208, 776, 228], \"page_id\": 102}], \"content\": \"(In thousands of dollars. Source: SF 220-8, compiled by Financial Management Service)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [24, 232, 1040, 323], \"page_id\": 102}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th rowspan=\\\"2\\\">Agency and program</th><th colspan=\\\"2\\\">Direct loans or credit</th><th colspan=\\\"2\\\">Guarantees or insurance</th></tr><tr><th>Amount outstanding</th><th>Maximum authority</th><th>Amount outstanding</th><th>Maximum authority</th></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [46, 325, 381, 372], \"page_id\": 102}], \"content\": \"I\\u2014Wholly owned Government enterprises U.S. dollar loans\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 377, 1038, 1322], \"page_id\": 102}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Funds appropriated to the President</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Guaranty reserve fund</td><td>524,493</td><td>829,367</td><td>\\u2014</td><td>\\u2014</td></tr><tr><td>Foreign military sales credit</td><td>8,519,673</td><td>13,346,330</td><td>8,741,013</td><td>9,623,842</td></tr><tr><td>Military sales credit 