P) grew at an annual rate of 3.8 percent. This was the fastest rate for any quarter in 4 years, but still left growth over the first seven quarters of the current recovery at a sluggish 2.2 percent. The major thrust for growth came from gains in private domestic final sales\\u2014at annual rates of 4.3 percent for consumer spending, 29.1 percent for homebuilding, and 9.7 percent for business capital investment. The major negative was a reduced rate of inventory building. Inflation measures were mixed for quarter. The fixed-weighted index for goods and services purchased in this country, including imports, was up at a 2.8 percent rate, while the fixed-weighted index for GDP, covering items produced in the U.S., was up at a faster-than-expected 3.2 percent rate. Both increases were larger than those in the third quarter, but much less than increases in recent years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [647, 521, 1337, 1062], \"page_id\": 13}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [446, 1145, 1121, 1180], \"page_id\": 13}], \"content\": \"Percent change, fourth quarter to fourth quarter\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [124, 1200, 1337, 1788], \"page_id\": 13}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [40, 38, 73, 82], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": \"4\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [534, 40, 909, 68], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": \"PROFILE OF THE ECONOMY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [33, 252, 776, 899], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [297, 917, 536, 959], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": \"FISCAL YEARS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"caption\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [124, 965, 658, 1012], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": \"\\\"Projections from 'The Economic and Budget Outlook: Fiscal Years 1994-1998,' prepared by the Congressional Budget Office\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [801, 256, 1309, 442], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": \"CHART POE-B.-- Federal Outlays and Receipts as a Percent of Gross Domestic Product\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [798, 457, 1391, 618], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": \"Congressional Budget Office estimates place fiscal 1993 outlays at 23.5 percent of Gross Domestic Product (GDP), and the receipts share at 18.5 percent, yielding a deficit share of 5 percent. (The Clinton Administration budget was not available at time of publication.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [801, 618, 1375, 722], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": \"The outlay share remains relatively high and is less than a percentage point below the peak in the early 1980's when the economy was especially weak and defense spending was rising.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [799, 722, 1386, 848], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": \"Despite cutbacks in discretionary programs, the outlay share is projected to be little changed by fiscal 1998 at 23.4 percent of GDP, as growth of mandatory programs and net interest continue to put pressure on the budget.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [804, 848, 1342, 903], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": \"The receipts share is projected to rise some to 18.8 percent of GDP in that year.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [798, 903, 1378, 1031], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": \"The gap between receipts and outlays is estimated at 4.5 percent of GDP by fiscal 1998. With the economy expected to be operating at a high level of employment, most of that figure represents a structural deficit.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [68, 1047, 1403, 1888], \"page_id\": 14}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [582, 54, 964, 92], \"page_id\": 15}], \"content\": \"PROFILE OF THE ECONOMY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1421, 51, 1448, 92], \"page_id\": 15}], \"content\": \"5\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [403, 369, 1133, 434], \"page_id\": 15}], \"content\": \"CHART POE-D.--Personal Saving\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [196, 440, 748, 729], \"page_id\": 15}], \"content\": \"Personal saving as a share of after-tax income was 4.8 percent in 1992, little changed from 4.7 percent in 1991, but up from a 4 percent low in 1989. Recent readings are significantly below yearly highs of 9 percent or more, as well as the 6.8 percent long-term average.The saving rate averaged 5.1 percent in first half 1992, but fell back to 4.6 percent in the second half, as the 4 percent annual rate growth in real consumer spending was almost\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [770, 447, 1317, 695], \"page_id\": 15}], \"content\": \"double the growth of real after-tax income. In December, the saving rate rose to 4.7 percent from 4.2 percent in November. The improvement was largely the result of an artificially strong 0.9 percent gain in real disposable income for the month. Excluding a series of special factors, income would have increased by only 0.25 percent in December and the saving rate would have been closer to 4 percent.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [406, 738, 1161, 787], \"page_id\": 15}], \"content\": \"(Household saving as a percent of after-tax income)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"caption\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [217, 800, 1320, 1820], \"page_id\": 15}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [20, 46, 36, 68], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"6\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [512, 42, 881, 66], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"PROFILE OF THE ECONOMY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [166, 610, 571, 647], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"CHARTS POE-E.--\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [222, 702, 571, 791], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"Real Disposable Personal Income\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [27, 817, 575, 972], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"Real disposable (after-tax) income of households rose by a sizable 0.9 percent in December, but most of that increase reflected special factors\\u2014particularly a shift of some income into December that normally would be taken in January. Excluding such factors, the rise was about\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [45, 970, 575, 1102], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"Excluding special factors, real spendable income grew at a moderate pace of about a 3 percent annual rate in the final quarter of 1992, after no change during the summer.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [610, 440, 1275, 1067], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [773, 1135, 1299, 1177], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"Real Consumer Spending\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [76, 1137, 715, 1168], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"(Percent change, fourth quarter to fourth quarter)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [48, 1204, 726, 1875], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [770, 1206, 1292, 1334], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"Real consumer spending was up 0.4 percent in December. For all of the fourth quarter, real spending rose by a 4.3 percent annual rate, on top of the 3.7 percent rise in the third quarter.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [773, 1334, 1295, 1438], \"page_id\": 16}], \"content\": \"A decline in the personal saving rate to 4.5 percent in the fourth quarter from 5.3 percent in the spring quarter of 1992 was a result of stronger increases in spending than in income\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [584, 63, 954, 94], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"PROFILE OF THE ECONOMY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1423, 65, 1445, 94], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"7\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [91, 273, 648, 364], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"CHARTS POE-F.-- Merchandise Trade Deficit\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [88, 389, 705, 601], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"The merchandise trade deficit widened to $7.6 billion in November, up from the revised $7.2 billion now posted for October. The trade gap widened in 1992, reflecting an improving U.S. economy and weakness overseas. For the first 11 months of the year, the trade deficit was at an annual rate of $82.5 billion, versus $65.5 billion during all of 1991. Both exports and imports were down in November. Exports dropped by 2.8\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [730, 389, 1342, 494], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"percent following sizable gains in the prior 2 months. Imports fell by 1.6 percent, including a decline of 6.6 percent in the oil import bill due to lower demand as well as lower prices.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [730, 494, 1338, 601], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"In real (1987 dollar) terms, the trade deficit for October and November averaged $79 billion at an annual rate, off significantly from the $91 billion of the third quarter of 1992.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [86, 651, 1332, 1185], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [86, 1241, 1219, 1757], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [111, 1826, 872, 1864], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"Jan. Feb. Mar. Apr. May June July Aug. Sept. Oct. Nov.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"caption\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [473, 1876, 534, 1905], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"1992\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"page_footer\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [23, 46, 38, 68], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"8\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [512, 42, 874, 66], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"PROFILE OF THE ECONOMY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [20, 404, 824, 444], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"CHARTS POE-G.--Leading Indicators\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [38, 486, 498, 660], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"The December index of leading indicators jumped by 1.9 percent, the largest monthly gain since April 1983. Early reports for January suggest another rise of that magnitude is not likely. The index had increased by a revised 0.7 percent in November and 0.5 percent in October.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [25, 667, 498, 870], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"The largest contribution to the increase came from higher consumer expectations and lower initial unemployment claims, both of which reversed in January. Seven other components of the leading index were positive, including new orders for consumer and capital goods. Two components were slightly negative.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [63, 875, 499, 948], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"The index of coincident indicators rose by 0.6 percent in December after no change in November and a similar 0.6\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [38, 952, 498, 1025], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"The lagging index dipped by 0.1 percent after a 0.9 percent jump in November.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [542, 481, 1265, 1087], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [20, 1135, 1272, 1756], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [435, 1769, 766, 1789], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"Shaded areas represent periods of recession.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [574, 60, 937, 83], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"PROFILE OF THE ECONOMY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1413, 63, 1425, 83], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [401, 369, 1131, 409], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"CHART POE-H.--Consumer Prices\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [214, 434, 740, 613], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"Consumer prices rose by 0.1 percent in December in a generally favorable price report for the month. During the 12 months of 1992, the Consumer Price Index (CPI) rose by 2.9 percent, off from 3.1 percent in 1991 and well below the increases in each of the prior 4 years, which were in excess of 4 percent.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [216, 615, 731, 691], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"In December, a slight rise in food prices was offset by a 0.6 percent dip in energy prices. The nonfood, nonenergy index, used to mea\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [788, 434, 1309, 537], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"ure \\\"core inflation,\\\" rose by 0.1 percent. During 1992 the index increased by 3.3 percent, the smallest gain for any year since 1972, when wage and price controls were in effect.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [788, 539, 1318, 666], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"Deceleration of growth of the nonfood, nonenergy index was broadly based in 1992. Some notable contributions to the slowdown included apparel, autos, medical care, and the costs of renting or owning a home.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [529, 713, 1042, 744], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"(Percent change from a year earlier)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [191, 767, 1312, 1824], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [32, 51, 60, 71], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"10\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [521, 46, 886, 71], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"PROFILE OF THE ECONOMY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [212, 380, 1194, 431], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"CHART POE-I.--Civilian Unemployment Rates\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [141, 468, 685, 682], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"The unemployment rate for January dipped 0.2 points to 7.1 percent, down 0.6 points from a high of 7.7 percent last June. The decline in the jobless rate was due to a 0.5 million drop in the labor force, or the number of job seekers plus those holding jobs. The results of the household survey, which tends to be volatile, indicate employment was also down in January. However, the\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [725, 466, 1270, 652], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"more reliable establishment survey registered a moderate increase. The number of persons counted as unemployed was 9 million for the month, down 775,000 from June. By race, the jobless rate was 6.2 percent for whites, 14.2 percent for blacks, including 39 percent for black teenagers, and 11.6 percent for Hispanics.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [149, 711, 1274, 1606], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [544, 47, 974, 89], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1393, 54, 1425, 87], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"11\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [388, 244, 1124, 284], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Federal Fiscal Operations\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [81, 324, 740, 539], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Budget authority usually takes the form of appropriations that allow obligations to be incurred and payments to be made. Reappropriations are Congressional actions that extend the availability of unobligated amounts that have expired or would otherwise expire. These are counted as new budget authority in the fiscal year of the legislation in which the reappropriation act is included, regardless of when the amounts were originally appropriated or when they would otherwise lapse.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [73, 543, 745, 702], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Obligations generally are liquidated by the issuance of checks or the disbursement of cash-outlays. Obligations may also be liquidated (and outlays recorded) by the accrual of interest on public issues of Treasury debt securities (including an increase in redemption value of bonds outstanding); or by the issuance of bonds, debentures, notes, monetary credits, or electronic payments.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [75, 706, 746, 921], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Refunds of collections generally are treated as reductions of collections, whereas payments for earned-income tax credits in excess of tax liabilities are treated as outlays. Outlays during a fiscal year may be for payment of obligations incurred in prior years or in the same year. Outlays, therefore, flow in part from unexpended balances of prior year budget authority and from budget authority provided for the year in which the money is spent. Total outlays include both budget and off-budget outlays and are stated net of offsetting collections.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [80, 928, 748, 1111], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Receipts are reported in the tables as either budget receipts or offsetting collections. They are collections from the public, excluding receipts offset against outlays. These, also called governmental receipts, consist mainly of tax receipts (including social insurance taxes), receipts from court fines, certain licenses, and deposits of earnings by the Federal Reserve system. Refunds of receipts are treated as deductions from gross receipts.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [76, 1118, 745, 1379], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Offsetting collections from other Government accounts or the public are of a business-type or market-oriented nature. They are classified as either collections credited to appropriations or fund accounts, or offsetting receipts (i.e., amounts deposited in receipt accounts). The former normally can be used without appropriation act by Congress. These occur in two instances: (1) when authorized by law, amounts collected for materials or services are treated as reimbursements to appropriations, and (2) in the three types of revolving funds (public enterprise, intragovernmental, and trust); collections are netted against spending, and outlays are reported as the net amount.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [76, 1384, 750, 1572], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Offsetting receipts in receipt accounts cannot be used without appropriation. They are subdivided into two categories: (1) proprietary receipts, or collections from the public, offset against outlays by agency and by function, and (2) intra-governmental funds, or payments into receipt accounts from governmental appropriation or fund accounts. They finance operations within and between Government agencies and are credited with collections from other Government accounts.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [76, 1580, 746, 1737], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Intrabudgetary transactions are subdivided into three categories: (1) interfund transactions--payments are from one fund group (either Federal funds or trust funds) to a receipt account in the other fund group; (2) Federal intrafund transactions--payments and receipts both occur within the Federal fund group; and (3) trust intrafund transactions--payments and receipts both occur within the trust fund group.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [80, 1741, 748, 1880], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Offsetting receipts are generally deducted from budget authority and outlays by function, subfunction, or agency. There are four types of receipts, however, that are deducted from budget totals as undistributed offsetting receipts. They are: (1) agencies' payments (including payments by off-budget Federal entities) as employers into employees\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [773, 326, 1433, 438], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"retirement funds; (2) interest received by trust funds; (3) rents and royalties on the Outer Continental Shelf lands; and (4) other interest (i.e., that collected on Outer Continental Shelf money in deposit funds when such money is transferred into the budget).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [776, 440, 1436, 682], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"The Government has used the unified budget concept as a foundation for its budgetary analysis and presentation since 1969. The concept calls for the budget to include all of the Government's fiscal transactions with the public. Since 1971, however, various laws have been enacted removing several Federal entities from (or creating them outside of) the budget. Other laws have moved certain off-budget Federal entities onto the budget. Under current law, the off-budget Federal entities consist of the two Social Security trust funds, Federal old-age and survivors insurance, and Federal disability insurance.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [778, 689, 1436, 896], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Although an off-budget Federal entity's receipts, outlays, and surplus or deficit ordinarily are not subject to targets set by the congressional resolution, the Balanced Budget and Emergency Deficit Control Act of 1985 (commonly known as the Gramm-Rudman-Hollings Act) included off-budget surplus or deficit in calculating deficit targets under that act and in calculating excess deficit. Partly for this reason, attention has focused on both on- and off-budget receipts, outlays, and deficit of the Government.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [776, 899, 1438, 1169], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Tables FFO-1, FFO-2, and FFO-3 are published quarterly and cover 5 years of data, estimates for 2 years, detail for 13 months, and fiscal year-to-date data. They provide a summary of data relating to Federal fiscal operations reported by Federal entities and disbursing officers, and daily reports from the Federal Reserve banks. They also detail accounting transactions affecting receipts and outlays of the Government and off-budget Federal entities and their related effect on assets and liabilities of the Government. Data are derived from the Monthly Treasury Statement of Receipts and Outlays of the United States Government.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [779, 1174, 1438, 1263], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Table FFO-1 summarizes the amount of total receipts, outlays, and surplus or deficit, as well as transactions in Federal securities, monetary assets, and balances in Treasury operating cash.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [779, 1270, 1440, 1379], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Table FFO-2 includes on- and off-budget receipts by source. Amounts represent income taxes, social insurance taxes, net contributions for other insurance and retirement, excise taxes, estate and gift taxes, customs duties, and net miscellaneous receipts.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [824, 1388, 1406, 1422], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Table FFO-3 details on- and off-budget outlays by agency.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [776, 1433, 1441, 1643], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Table FFO-4 (Fall issue) summarizes internal revenue collections by States and other areas and by type of tax. Amounts reported are collections made in a fiscal year. They span several tax liability years because they consist of prepayments (i.e., estimated tax payments and taxes withheld by employers for individual income and Social Security taxes), of payments made with tax returns, and of subsequent payments made after tax returns are due or are filed (i.e., payments with delinquent returns or on delinquent accounts).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [778, 1652, 1438, 1862], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"It is important to note that these data do not necessarily reflect the Federal tax burden of individual States. Amounts are reported based on the primary filing address provided by each taxpayer or reporting entity. For multistate corporations, the address may reflect only the State where such a corporation reported its taxes from a principal office rather than other States where income was earned or where individual income and Social Security taxes were withheld. In addition, an individual may reside in one State and work in another.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 19, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [35, 42, 66, 68], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"12\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [504, 42, 920, 71], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [323, 232, 1105, 272], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"Budget Results for the First Quarter, Fiscal 1993\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [35, 334, 207, 364], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"SUMMARY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [35, 398, 693, 669], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"The Federal budget deficit totaled $120.5 billion in the first quarter of fiscal 1993. This was $36.7 billion wider than the $83.8 billion in the corresponding quarter of fiscal 1992. About $25 billion of that widening reflected the unusual timing of payments this fiscal year. Because of the long New Year's holiday weekend this year, January Social Security checks went out in December, ballooning the outlay total for that month and for the entire first fiscal quarter. Outlays in January and in the second fiscal quarter will be correspondingly smaller.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [35, 682, 697, 1060], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"Two other special factors affected the deficit and outlay totals in the first fiscal quarter compared with a year earlier, but these had largely offsetting impacts. Deposit insurance outlays were negative by $7.75 billion in the quarter, as asset sales (negative outlays) by the Resolution Trust Corporation and other deposit insurance agencies exceeded gross spending. This compares with negative outlays of about $4.75 billion in the same quarter a year earlier. Foreign contributions to the Defense Cooperation Account (in support of the Desert Storm operation) had been $3.6 billion in the first quarter of fiscal 1992. These are treated as negative outlays in budget accounting and helped hold down the deficit in that quarter. Such contributions were negligible in the first quarter of this fiscal year.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [35, 1073, 697, 1162], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"Excluding these special factors, and including the unusual timing of payments, the deficit was wider in the first quarter of this fiscal year by $11 billion than a year earlier.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [38, 1177, 697, 1367], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"Total outlays increased from a year earlier by 6.1 percent in the first fiscal quarter, after adjustment for the special factors noted above. That is a little faster than the increases projected for the entire fiscal year by either the Congressional Budget Office or the Bush Administration. (Projections of the new Administration were not available at this writing.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [40, 1380, 698, 1654], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"Among functional categories of outlays, defense spending increased from a year earlier by 0.6 percent (excluding the Defense Cooperation Account). Defense outlays are projected to decline by about 4.5 percent for the entire fiscal year, and the increase for the first quarter appears, at least, partly to reflect some special outlays connected with operations in Somalia and in the Middle East. Net interest outlays decreased by 1.1 percent, as a decline in interest rates more than offset an 11.25 percent increase in the publicly held debt outstanding.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 1668, 698, 1729], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"Among spending categories registering sizable increases in the quarter, outlays for income support were up\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [731, 331, 1390, 1005], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [736, 1038, 1393, 1279], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"from a year earlier by 12.6 percent, including a rise of 36.4 percent in unemployment insurance benefits. Much of the latter rise reflects availability of extended benefits. Agriculture-function spending rose by 22.5 percent, reflecting favorable crops and soft crop prices. Outlays for Medicare and other health care rose by a moderate 7.7 percent. However, these are projected to increase substantially faster than that for the entire fiscal year, though not by as much as the 18.5 percent increase for all of fiscal 1992.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [736, 1294, 1395, 1725], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"Total revenues rose by 4 percent in the first fiscal quarter from a year earlier. This is a little less than projected for the entire fiscal year by either the Congressional Budget Office or the Bush Administration, but that apparent shortfall could easily be made up later in the fiscal year. The increase for the quarter was led by a rise of 11.9 percent in net corporate income tax payments. One notable feature of the results for the quarter was a strong flow of withheld individual income and employment taxes in the final few days of December. This was apparently in response to bonus income and stock options that were taken in calendar 1992 in anticipation of possible increases in marginal income tax rates. That added revenue flow remained strong in January, including a large increase in estimated individual income tax payments, and should help boost the revenue total for the current fiscal year.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [551, 65, 967, 101], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1396, 67, 1431, 94], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"13\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [81, 248, 461, 300], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Fourth-Quarter Receipts\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [83, 322, 728, 490], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"The following capsule analysis of budget receipts, by source, for the fourth quarter of fiscal 1992 supplements fiscal data reported in the December issue of the \\\"Treasury Bulletin.\\\" At the time of that issue's release, not enough data was available to analyze adequately collections for the quarter.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [80, 514, 743, 870], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Individual income taxes\\u2014Individual income tax receipts were $125.3 billion for the fourth quarter of fiscal 1992. This represents an increase of $4.4 billion over the comparable quarter for fiscal 1991. Withheld receipts decreased by $1.1 billion for this period, although the quarterly reconciliation between the general fund and the Social Security and Medicare trust funds increased by $0.2 billion. Non-withheld receipts increased by $5.1 billion over the comparable quarter of fiscal 1991, of which $0.2 billion is an increase in the quarterly reconciliation between the general fund and the Social Security and Medicare trust funds. Refunds decreased by $0.4 billion over the comparable quarter.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [80, 881, 741, 1102], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Corporate income taxes\\u2014Corporate receipts for the fourth quarter of fiscal 1992 totaled $24.3 billion. This was $2.6 billion higher than receipts for the comparable quarter of fiscal 1991. The $2.6 billion figure is composed of $2.3 billion in additional estimated and final payments plus $0.4 billion in lower refunds. The increase in net receipts reflects both increased corporate profits and new estimated payment rules that became effective in June.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [78, 1109, 745, 1413], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Employment taxes and contributions\\u2014Employment taxes and contributions receipts for the July-September 1992 quarter were $91.1 billion, an increase of $1.6 billion over the comparable prior-year quarter. Receipts to the old-age survivors insurance, the disability insurance, and the hospital insurance trust funds increased by $1.7 billion, $0.2 billion, and $0.5 billion, respectively. Refunds were $0.8 billion higher than the prior-year quarter. The overall increase in employment taxes and contributions receipts from the prior year offsets a $0.5 billion decrease in accounting adjustments for previous years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [783, 259, 1438, 503], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Contributions for other insurance and retirement--Contributions for other retirement were $1.2 billion for the fourth quarter of fiscal 1992. There was a negligible change in receipts from the fourth quarter of fiscal 1991. The growth in contributions will remain flat over the next few years as the number of employees covered by the Federal employees' retirement system (FERS) grows slowly relative to those covered under the civil service retirement system (CSRS).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [779, 514, 1435, 731], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Unemployment insurance\\u2014Unemployment insurance receipts for the fourth quarter of fiscal 1992 were $5.3 billion compared with $5.2 billion for the comparable quarter of fiscal 1991. The increase primarily reflects an increase of $0.7 billion in State taxes deposited in the U.S. Treasury. Combined Federal Unemployment Tax Act Receipts (FUTA). Railroad unemployment tax receipts fell $0.1 billion.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [778, 749, 1435, 1046], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Excise taxes\\u2014Excise tax receipts for the July-September 1992 quarter were $11.7 billion compared to $12.9 billion for the comparable quarter of fiscal 1991, a decrease of $1.2 billion over the prior-year level. Refunds increased by $0.4 billion. The remainder of the decrease in receipts is due to an unusually high level of excise tax receipts in the July-September 1991 quarter. This high level may have been the result of changes in excise tax deposit rules that, when combined with the excise tax increases enacted as part of the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1990, resulted in shifting excise tax receipts between quarters.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [778, 1055, 1433, 1223], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Estate and gift taxes\\u2014Estate and gift tax receipts were $2.8 billion for the July-September quarter of fiscal 1992. This represents a decrease of $0.1 billion over the comparable prior-year quarter. Total receipts for fiscal 1992 were $11.1 billion with a negligible increase over fiscal 1991 receipts.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [783, 1234, 1433, 1355], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Customs duties\\u2014Customs receipts net of refunds were $4.8 billion for the fourth quarter of fiscal 1992. This is an increase of $0.5 billion from the comparable prior-year quarter. It is due to an increase in imports.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [783, 1361, 1435, 1480], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Miscellaneous receipts\\u2014Net miscellaneous receipts for the fourth quarter of fiscal 1992 were $8.5 billion, an increase of $3.1 billion over the comparable prior-year quarter.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [337, 1540, 1161, 1574], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Fourth Quarter Fiscal 1992 Net Budget Receipts, by Source\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [672, 1574, 824, 1592], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"(In billions of dollars)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [121, 1592, 1385, 1864], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th>Source</th><th>July</th><th>August</th><th>September</th></tr><tr><td>Individual income taxes</td><td>35.1</td><td>34.7</td><td>55.5</td></tr><tr><td>Corporate income taxes</td><td>2.6</td><td>1.6</td><td>19.9</td></tr><tr><td>Employment taxes and contributions</td><td>29.5</td><td>29.0</td><td>32.6</td></tr><tr><td>Unemployment insurance</td><td>1.8</td><td>3.8</td><td>0.3</td></tr><tr><td>Contributions for other insurance and retirement</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.4</td></tr><tr><td>Excise taxes</td><td>3.5</td><td>4.1</td><td>4.1</td></tr><tr><td>Estate and gift taxes</td><td>0.9</td><td>0.8</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>Customs duties</td><td>1.7</td><td>1.8</td><td>1.6</td></tr><tr><td>Miscellaneous receipts</td><td>3.2</td><td>2.3</td><td>3.0</td></tr><tr><td>Total budget receipts</td><td>78.9</td><td>78.3</td><td>118.4</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 46, 80, 80], \"page_id\": 24}], \"content\": \"14\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [516, 44, 930, 82], \"page_id\": 24}], \"content\": \"FEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [46, 241, 1302, 294], \"page_id\": 24}], \"content\": \"CHART FFO-A.--Monthly Receipts and Outlays, Fiscal 1992\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 309, 299, 342], \"page_id\": 24}], \"content\": \"(In billions of dollars)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [43, 422, 363, 998], \"page_id\": 24}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bb