tions of the United States with remaining periods to maturity comparable to the maturities of such investments, except that the interest rate on such investments shall not exceed the average interest rate applicable to existing borrowings.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [745, 437, 1398, 532], \"page_id\": 150}], \"content\": \"An annual report to Congress by the Secretary of the Treasury, after consultation with the Secretary of Energy, is required by 42 U.S.C. 10222 (e) (1). This report must present the financial condition and the results of operations of the waste fund during the preceding fiscal year.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [469, 676, 977, 742], \"page_id\": 150}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-18.--Nuclear Waste Fund Results of Operations, Fiscal 1992\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [580, 773, 869, 793], \"page_id\": 150}], \"content\": \"(Source: Financial Management Service)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [48, 864, 1400, 1758], \"page_id\": 150}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Balance Oct. 1, 1991</td><td>$2,117,770</td></tr><tr><td>Receipts</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Fees collected</td><td>573,848,718</td></tr><tr><td>Penalties and interest on fee payments</td><td>51</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments</td><td>291,990,358</td></tr><tr><td>Total receipts</td><td>865,839,127</td></tr><tr><td>Outlays:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Department of Energy radioactive waste disposal activities</td><td>346,257,495</td></tr><tr><td>Cost of investments</td><td>520,090,306</td></tr><tr><td>Total outlays</td><td>866,347,801</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Sept. 30, 1992</td><td>1,609,096</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [660, 83, 851, 105], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"TRUST FUNDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1382, 83, 1425, 103], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"141\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [396, 275, 1114, 306], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Reforestation Trust Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [83, 364, 733, 481], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"The Reforestation Trust Fund was established on the books of the Treasury in fiscal 1981 to continue through September 30, 1985, according to provisions of Title III-Reforestation, of the Recreational Boating Safety and Facilities Improvement Act of 1980 [16 U.S.C. 1606a (a)].\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [85, 487, 736, 673], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"The act provides that the Secretary of the Treasury shall transfer to the trust fund tariffs, limited to not more than $30 million for any fiscal year, received in the Treasury from October 1, 1979, through September 30, 1985, on (1) rough and primary wood products and wood waste; (2) lumber, flooring, and moldings; and (3) wood veneers, plywood, and other wood-veneer assemblies, and building boards. Public Law 99-190 extended the receipts for the trust fund. Amounts available in the reforestation trust fund exceeding current withdrawals are invested in\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [778, 364, 1425, 452], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"interest-bearing obligations of the United States or in obligations guaranteed as to both principal and interest by the United States. The interest on, and the proceeds from the sale or redemption of, any obligations are credited to the trust fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [778, 465, 1425, 554], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"The Secretary of Agriculture is authorized to obligate available sums in the trust fund (including any amounts not obligated in previous years) for (1) reforestation and timber stand improvement and (2) administrative costs of the Government for these activities.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [779, 566, 1428, 680], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"Annual reports are required by 16 U.S.C. 1606 a (c) (1) to be submitted by the Secretary of the Treasury, after consultation with the Secretary of Agriculture, on the financial condition and the results of the operations of the trust fund during the past fiscal year and on its expected condition and operations during the next fiscal year.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [469, 780, 1047, 845], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-19.--Reforestation Trust Fund Results of Operations, Fiscal 1992\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [612, 874, 901, 892], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"(Source: Financial Management Service)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [85, 925, 1431, 1308], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Balance Oct. 1, 1991</td><td>$7,745,413</td></tr><tr><td>Receipts:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Excise taxes (tariffs)</td><td>30,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Redemption of investment</td><td>8,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Total receipts</td><td>38,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Expenses:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Expenditure</td><td>31,037,350</td></tr><tr><td>Total expenses</td><td>31,037,350</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Sept. 30, 1992</td><td>14,708,063</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [362, 1444, 1158, 1478], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"Expected Condition and Results of Operations, Fiscal 1993\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [677, 1504, 844, 1522], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"(In thousands of dollars)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [85, 1536, 1431, 1893], \"page_id\": 151}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Balance Oct 1, 1992</td><td>14,708</td></tr><tr><td>Receipts:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Excise taxes (tariffs)</td><td>30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments</td><td>300</td></tr><tr><td>Total receipts</td><td>30,300</td></tr><tr><td>Outlays</td><td>30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Sept. 30, 1993</td><td>15,508</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [40, 46, 93, 77], \"page_id\": 152}], \"content\": \"142\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [625, 40, 818, 75], \"page_id\": 152}], \"content\": \"TRUST FUNDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [299, 342, 1148, 389], \"page_id\": 152}], \"content\": \"CHARTS TF-F and G.--Major Trust Funds\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [481, 440, 736, 479], \"page_id\": 152}], \"content\": \"(In billions of dollars)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [153, 488, 983, 1137], \"page_id\": 152}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1005, 928, 1290, 1071], \"page_id\": 152}], \"content\": \"Interest on Investments, Fiscal 1991-92\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [697, 1153, 950, 1199], \"page_id\": 152}], \"content\": \"(In billions of dollars)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [156, 1202, 1309, 1807], \"page_id\": 152}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [662, 40, 852, 69], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"TRUST FUNDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1383, 40, 1428, 72], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"143\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [352, 235, 1159, 273], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Aquatic Resources Trust Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [83, 322, 746, 416], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"Aquatic Resources Trust Fund was established on the books of the Treasury pursuant to the Deficit Reduction Act of 1984 (Public Law 98-369, approved July 18, 1984). The fund consists of two accounts: the Sport Fish Restoration Account and Boat Safety Account.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [80, 440, 745, 512], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"Motorboat fuel taxes are transferred, in amounts up to $70 million per fiscal year, from the Highway Trust Fund to the Boat Safety Account. Authority for such transfers expires on October 1, 1997.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [81, 530, 746, 787], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"Amounts equivalent to the excise taxes received on sport fishing equipment and import duties on fishing tackle, yachts and pleasure craft are appropriated into the Sport Fish Restoration Account. Motorboat fuel taxes not transferred to the Boat Safety Account or to the Land and Water Conservation Fund are transferred from the Highway Trust Fund to the Sport Fish Restoration Account. Transfers of motorboat fuel taxes from the Highway Trust Fund to the Land and Water Conservation Fund are limited to $1 million per fiscal year. In addition, pursuant to the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1990 (Public Law 101-508), small engine gasoline taxes are transferred to the Sport Fish Restoration Account.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [768, 320, 1428, 487], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"Amounts in the Sport Fish Restoration Account are used, as provided by appropriation acts, for the purposes of carrying out \\\"An Act to provide that the United States shall aid the States in fish restoration and management projects, and for other purposes,\\\" approved August 9, 1950 (as in effect on October 1, 1988) and the Coastal Wetlands Planning, Protection and Restoration Act, as in effect on November 29, 1990.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [770, 492, 1431, 563], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"Amounts in the Boat Safety Account, as provided by appropriations acts are available to carry out the purposes of Public Law 92-75 related to recreational boating safety assistance.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [768, 572, 1431, 666], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"When the provisions of 26 U.S.C. 9602 (b) are met, amounts available in the two accounts exceeding outlay requirements are invested in public debt securities with the interest credited to the respective accounts.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [768, 675, 1435, 794], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"Annual reports to Congress, required by 26 U.S.C. 9602 (a), are submitted by the Secretary of the Treasury. These reports are required to cover the financial condition and results of operations of the fund during the past fiscal year and those expected during the next 5 fiscal years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [431, 874, 1083, 939], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-20.--Aquatic Resources Trust Fund Sport Fish Restoration\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"caption\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [617, 966, 899, 993], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"(Source: Financial Management Service)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [80, 1017, 1436, 1804], \"page_id\": 153}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Balance Oct. 1, 1991</td><td>$510,753,089</td></tr><tr><td>Revenue:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Gas, motorboat</td><td>73,388,000</td></tr><tr><td>Fish equipment</td><td>76,636,000</td></tr><tr><td>Electric trolling sonar</td><td>1,971,000</td></tr><tr><td>Import duty equipment, yacht</td><td>26,930,000</td></tr><tr><td>Gas, small engines</td><td>49,500,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments</td><td>34,853,653</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments\\u2014accrued</td><td>-1,895,065</td></tr><tr><td>Total revenue</td><td>261,383,588</td></tr><tr><td>Nonexpenditure transfers:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Interior</td><td>236,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Corps of Engineers</td><td>3,200,000</td></tr><tr><td>Total nonexpenditure transfers</td><td>239,200,000</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Sept. 30, 1992</td><td>532,936,677</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [38, 24, 81, 49], \"page_id\": 154}], \"content\": \"144\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [617, 22, 804, 49], \"page_id\": 154}], \"content\": \"TRUST FUNDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [386, 221, 1033, 283], \"page_id\": 154}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-20.--Aquatic Resources Trust Fund Boat Safety\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [569, 314, 854, 331], \"page_id\": 154}], \"content\": \"(Source: Financial Management Service)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [36, 364, 1391, 983], \"page_id\": 154}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Balance, Oct. 1, 1991</td><td>$62,218,679</td></tr><tr><td>Revenue:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Gas, motorboat</td><td>70,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments</td><td>3,610,502</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments-accrued</td><td>-16,958</td></tr><tr><td>Total revenue</td><td>73,593,544</td></tr><tr><td>Nonexpenditure transfers:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Boat safety</td><td>74,314,292</td></tr><tr><td>Total nonexpenditure transfers</td><td>74,314,292</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Sept. 30, 1992</td><td>61,497,931</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 1027, 702, 1045], \"page_id\": 154}], \"content\": \"Note.--The expected conditions and results of operations for fiscal years 1993 through 1997\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 1043, 352, 1056], \"page_id\": 154}], \"content\": \"wera not available at tha time of publication.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [652, 42, 851, 76], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"TRUST FUNDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1370, 40, 1428, 85], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"145\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [350, 232, 1154, 286], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [68, 322, 745, 447], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"The Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund was established on the books of the Treasury by the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act 1986 (Public Law 99-509). It was made effective on January 1, 1990 by the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1989 (Public Law 101-239), and amended by the Oil Pollution Act of 1990 (Public Law 101-380).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [66, 454, 745, 686], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"Amounts equivalent to the taxes received from the environmental tax on petroleum, but only to the extent of the Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund rate, are appropriated to the fund. The tax is not applied during any calendar quarter if the Secretary of the Treasury estimates that, as of the close of the preceding calendar quarter, the unobligated balance in the fund exceeds $1 billion. According to Treasury projections, the fund's unobligated balance will exceed $1 billion at the close of a calendar quarter for the first time on either March 31, 1993, or June 30, 1993. When that happens, the tax will not apply in the following calendar quarter.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 322, 1428, 418], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"Certain amounts were transferred from other funds and were appropriated to the Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund as provided by U.S.C. 26 9509 (b). certain paid penalties a nd amounts recovered for damages are also appropriated to the fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 429, 1433, 503], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"Amounts in the fund are available for oil spill cleanup costs and certain other related purposes as provided by appropriations acts or section 6002 (b) of the Oil Pollution Act of 1990.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [758, 512, 1436, 581], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"When the provisions of 26 U.S.C. 9602 (b) are met, amounts available in the fund exceeding outlay requirements are invested in public debt securities. Interest is credited to the fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [763, 592, 1435, 689], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"Annual reports to Congress, required by 26 U.S.C. (a), are submitted by the Secretary of the Treasury. These reports are required to cover the financial condition and results of operations of the fund during the past fiscal year and those expected during the next 5 fiscal years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [430, 807, 1076, 856], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-21.--Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [600, 876, 906, 901], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"(Source: Financial Management Service)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [65, 970, 1433, 1800], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Balance Oct. 1, 1991</td><td>$618,390,474</td></tr><tr><td>Revenue:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Tax on crude oil/petro product</td><td>295,397,000</td></tr><tr><td>Expired $.03 barrel tax-Internal Revenue Service</td><td>-201,869</td></tr><tr><td>Cost recoveries</td><td>14,138,191</td></tr><tr><td>Fines and penalties</td><td>1,916,129</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments</td><td>41,126,573</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments-accrued</td><td>-531,714</td></tr><tr><td>Total revenue</td><td>351,844,310</td></tr><tr><td>Outlays/nonexpenditure transfers:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Environmental Protection Agency expense</td><td>18,433,800</td></tr><tr><td>Transfer to Coast Guard</td><td>81,308,618</td></tr><tr><td>Total outlays/nonexpenditure transfers</td><td>99,742,418</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Sept. 30, 1992</td><td>870,492,366</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [71, 1822, 746, 1867], \"page_id\": 155}], \"content\": \"Note. - The expected conditions and results of operations for fiscal years 1993 through 1997 were not available at the time of publication.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [40, 35, 85, 55], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"146\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [620, 27, 806, 51], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"TRUST FUNDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [221, 212, 1209, 269], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Vaccine Injury Compensation Trust Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 311, 698, 477], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"The Vaccine Injury Compensation Trust Fund (VICTF) was created on the books of the Treasury by the Revenue Act of 1987 (Public Law 100-203, approved December 22, 1987). Excise taxes on DPT, DT, MMR and polio vaccines (26 U.S.C. 4131) are appropriated into the fund which serves as a source of funds to pay claims for compensation for vaccine-related injury or death occurring after October 1, 1988. Under current law, the excise tax imposed by the VICTF expires if the project\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [731, 300, 1385, 353], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"balance of the VICTF, as of the calendar quarter ending December 31, 1992, is not negative.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [730, 358, 1388, 459], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"Annual reports to Congress, required by 26 U.S.C. (a), are submitted by the Secretary of the Treasury. These reports are required to cover the financial condition and results of operations of the fund during the past fiscal year and those expected during the next 5 fiscal years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [317, 532, 1119, 570], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-22.--Vaccine Injury Compensation Trust Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [574, 588, 866, 621], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"(Source: Financial Management Service)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [46, 643, 1395, 1100], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Balance Oct. 1, 1991</td><td>$463,348,741</td></tr><tr><td>Receipts:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Excise tax receipts</td><td>117,788,100</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments</td><td>13,947,519</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments-accrued</td><td>13,844,732</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [78, 1113, 1395, 1166], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Total receipts</td><td>145,580,351</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [48, 1281, 282, 1299], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"Outlays/nonexpenditure transfers\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [50, 1341, 1403, 1599], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>U.S. Claims Court expenses</td><td>1,685,650</td></tr><tr><td>U.S. Department of Justice</td><td>2,625,838</td></tr><tr><td>Health resources/transfers</td><td>17,887,746</td></tr><tr><td>Total outlays/norexpenditure transfers</td><td>22,199,335</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 1707, 1398, 1752], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Balance Sept. 30, 1982</td><td>586,729,757</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 1807, 715, 1846], \"page_id\": 156}], \"content\": \"Note.\\u2014The expected conditions and results of operations for fiscal years 1993 through 1997 were not available at the time of publication.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [662, 29, 856, 65], \"page_id\": 157}], \"content\": \"TRUST FUNDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1375, 31, 1446, 69], \"page_id\": 157}], \"content\": \"147\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [277, 217, 1252, 266], \"page_id\": 157}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: National Recreational Trails Trust Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [76, 306, 758, 545], \"page_id\": 157}], \"content\": \"Provisions relating to a National Recreational Trails Trust Fund were enacted as part of the Intermodal Surface Transportation Efficiency Act of 1991 (Public Law 102-240, approved December 18, 1991). The Act provides that amounts equivalent to the taxes imposed on fuel used in vehicles on recreational trails or back country terrain and in camp stoves and other non-engine uses in outdoor recreational equipment shall be paid from the Highway Trust Fund to the National Recreational Trails Trust Fund (26 U.S.C. 9503 (c) (6). Transfers to the fund are limited to amounts obligated under section 1302 of the Act. Obligated amounts cannot exceed $30 million per fiscal year over the\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [761, 313, 1438, 367], \"page_id\": 157}], \"content\": \"1992-97 period. Obligations cannot be made until amounts are appropriated; there have been no appropriations for either fiscal 1992 or 1993.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [771, 371, 1443, 443], \"page_id\": 157}], \"content\": \"When the provisions of 26 U.S.C. 9602 (b) are met, amounts available in the fund exceeding outlay requirements are invested in public debt securities with the interest credited to the fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [765, 445, 1443, 570], \"page_id\": 157}], \"content\": \"Annual reports to Congress, required by 26 U.S.C. 9602 (a), are submitted by the Secretary of the Treasury. These reports are required to cover the financial condition and results of operations of the fund during the past fiscal year and those expected during the next 5 fiscal years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [65, 570, 760, 639], \"page_id\": 157}], \"content\": \"Note.-- No current figures or future projections are available because funds have yet to be appropriated for the fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [451, 143, 964, 825], \"page_id\": 159}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [620, 42, 894, 74], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"TECHNICAL PAPERS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1382, 45, 1425, 72], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"151\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [527, 239, 982, 284], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"Recent Reports and Studies\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [504, 317, 1005, 362], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"Treasury Reviews Insurance Issues\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [76, 385, 738, 702], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"The Department of the Treasury's Office of Economic Policy recently released two research papers as part of a broad review of insurance issues. Research Paper No. 9202, \\\"Life-Health Insurance Markets,\\\" by John S. Greenlees and James E. Duggan, provides an economic overview of the life-health sector of the U.S. insurance market. This sector provides a mix of products and services to firms and individuals including annuities and pension fund management. According to the authors, changes in revenues and profitability in these product markets could have \\\"significant implications for the future prospects of the insurance industry.\\\" This paper presents information on the size and growth of the insurance industry, illustrating its role within the larger economy. It also includes a\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [775, 385, 1430, 438], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"discussion of health insurance issues and of the role of commercial life-health firms in the health insurance market.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [776, 436, 1433, 706], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"In Research Paper No. 9203, \\\"Property-Casualty Insurance Markets,\\\" authors Lucy Huffman and David Bernstein review financial trends and public policy issues affecting the property-casualty insurance industry. The study offers an overview of the structure and financial condition of the industry and discusses economic and legal factors that raise public policy issues and prompt solvency concerns, including the impact of the tort liability system on costs and nonmarket regulatory responses. The study also looks at competition in the insurance industry and summarizes performance issues in selected individual lines.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [431, 751, 1071, 794], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"Report on Social Security and the Public Debt\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [71, 811, 736, 943], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"For the next 25 years, the social security program is expected to have average surpluses of .6 percent of Gross National Product (GNP), according to James E. Duggan's Research Paper No. 9102. After that, the senior economist says, deficits will reach 1.7 percent of GNP (4 percent after the\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [775, 811, 1430, 870], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"addition of health care). These deficits could result in large, unstable debt ratios and may affect future U.S. debt policy.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [771, 870, 1433, 968], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"The study stresses the public debt implications of the long-run financial status of the program and presents three alternatives, or combinations thereof, for financing Social Security obligations.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [347, 997, 1166, 1035], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"Revenue Impact of Proposed Capital Gains Tax Reductions\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [71, 1060, 740, 1234], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"In recent years, a considerable amount of debate has centered on the likely effect of a decrease in the capital gains tax. While analysis of the issue has been split between two approaches\\u2014estimating aggregate responsiveness of capital gains realizations, as well as focusing on individual taxpayer responsiveness\\u2014neither has provided conclusive evidence to decide the issue.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [78, 1234, 738, 1361], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"Research Paper No. 9003, \\\"The effect of Marginal Tax Rates on Capital Gains Revenue: Another Look at the Evidence,\\\" by Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees, focuses on aspects of the debate. The authors define the responsiveness of revenue to tax rates at the individual and aggregate\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [771, 1060, 1435, 1113], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"levels. Citing an analysis by the Congressional Budget Office they present an econometric data analysis procedure.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [771, 1116, 1430, 1214], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"The study also includes time-series evidence incorporating revisions in the National Accounts and Flow of Funds data to demonstrate the effect of the Tax Reform Act of 1986 on the estimated relationship.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [773, 1214, 1433, 1361], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"The aim of the paper is to give a better understanding of the relationship among capital gains tax realizations, revenues, and tax rates. And although analyses do not give conclusive evidence on the effect of proposed tax rate changes, the authors conclude that the evidence does not suggest that a tax reduction would decrease tax revenues.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [78, 1424, 736, 1648], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"A related study concludes that a capital gains tax reduction would increase the number and amounts of such gains declared by taxpayers. And it would do so in sufficient amounts to increase tax revenues. Research Paper No. 9004, \\\"An Econometric Model of Capital Gains Realization Behavior,\\\" by Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees, and by Kimberly D. Zieschang (Office of Prices, Bureau of Labor Statistics), explores the revenue impact of proposed reductions in capital gains taxation, as well as the expected response of taxpayers.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [71, 1670, 740, 1800], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"The marginal tax rate on long-term gains has a negative impact on both the proportion of taxpayers realizing capital gains and on the value of those gains declared, according to the study. The researchers further stated that there was no evidence that income switching as a result of the reductions\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [773, 1428, 1433, 1504], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"would offset expected tax revenue increases. The study includes taxpayer data covering three historical tax policy regimes that varied widely in their treatment of capital gains.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 19, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [776, 1504, 1433, 1701], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"The authors supported their predictions by citing a 1988 study finding, in the past, the majority of capital gains were never realized for tax purposes. That 1988 study found only 3.1 percent of the stock of accrued gains realized each year between 1960-84. This large flow of unrealized gains, according to the authors, supports their conclusion that a reduction in the capital gains tax would yield a permanent increase in government revenues.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 20, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [773, 1701, 1430, 1753], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"(For related studies on the capital gains tax issue, see Research Paper Nos. 8801 and 9002.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 21, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [78, 1815, 1430, 1864], \"page_id\": 161}], \"content\": \"1 Copies may be obtained by writing to Shirley Bryant, Department of the Treasury, 1500 Pennsylvania Ave., NW., Room 4422, Washington, D.C. 20220; telephone number (202) 622-2010.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 22, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [36, 27, 101, 60], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"152\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [579, 18, 874, 60], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"TECHNICAL PAPERS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [526, 236, 904, 294], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"Research Paper Series\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [187, 347, 1247, 393], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"Available Through the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Economic Policy\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [36, 468, 1408, 546], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"8701. \\\"The Empirical Reliability of Monetary Aggregates as Indicators: 1983-1987.\\\" Michael R. Darby, Angelo R. Mascaro, and Michael L. Marlow.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [36, 574, 1406, 647], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"8702. \\\"The Impact of Government Deficits on Personal and National Saving Rates.\\\" Michael R. Darby, Robert Gillingham, and John S. Greenlees.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [36, 678, 1378, 720], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"8703. \\\"The Ins and Outs of Unemployment: The Ins Win.\\\" Michael R. Darby, John C. Haltiwanger, and Mark W. Plant\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [36, 753, 1405, 822], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"8704. \\\"Accounting for the Deficit: An Analysis of Sources of Change in the Federal and Total Government Deficits.\\\" Michael R. Darby.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [35, 857, 1401, 932], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"8801. \\\"The Direct Revenue Effects of Capital Gains Taxation: A Reconsideration of the Time Series Evidence.\\\" Michael R. Darby, Robert Gillingham, and John S. Greenlees.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [36, 963, 1007, 1012], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"9001. \\\"Some Economic Aspects of the U.S. Health Care System.\\\" James E. Duggan.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [35, 1034, 1139, 1084], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"9002. \\\"Historical Trends in the U.S. Cost of Capital.\\\" Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [35, 1120, 1405, 1184], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"9003. \\\"The Effect of Marginal Tax Rates on Capital Gains Revenue: Another Look at the Evidence.\\\" Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [36, 1213, 1405, 1288], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"9004. \\\"An Econometric Model of Capital Gains Realization Behavior.\\\" Robert Gillingham, John S. Greenlees, and Kimberly D. Zieschang.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [36, 1321, 1405, 1394], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"9101. \\\"The Impact of Government Deficits on Personal and National Saving Rates.\\\" (Revised) Michael R. Darby, Robert Gillingham, and John S. Greenlees.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [40, 1436, 768, 1471], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"9102. \\\"Social Security and the Public Debt.\\\" James E. Duggan.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 1506, 930, 1555], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"9201. \\\"Issues in Eastern European Social Security Reform.\\\" John C. Hambor.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [36, 1581, 964, 1623], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"9202. \\\"Life-Health Insurance Markets.\\\" John S. Greenlees and James E. Duggan\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [40, 1657, 990, 1701], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"9203. \\\"Property-Casualty Insurance Markets.\\\" Lucy Huffman and David Bernstein\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [68, 1754, 1377, 1820], \"page_id\": 162}], \"content\": \"Copies of these research papers may be obtained by writing to Shirley Bryant, U.S. Department of the Treasury, 16th & Pennsylvania Avenue, NW., Room 4422, Washington, D.C. 20220; telephone (202) 622-2010.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"page_footer\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1390, 42, 1436, 67], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"153\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [642, 217, 886, 282], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Glossary\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [287, 315, 1246, 358], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Expanded, With References to Applicable Sections and Tables\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [88, 414, 748, 572], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Accrued discount (SBN-1, -2, -3)\\u2014Interest that accumulates on savings bonds from the date of purchase until the date of redemption or final maturity, whichever comes first. Series A, B, C, D, E, EE, F, and J are discount or accrual type bonds\\u2014meaning principal and interest are paid when bonds are redeemed. Series G, H, HH, and K are current-income bonds, and the semiannual interest paid to their holders is not included in accrued discount.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [88, 581, 750, 691], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Amounts outstanding and in circulation (USCC)\\u2014Includes all issues by the Bureau of the Mint purposely intended as a medium of exchange. Coins sold by the Bureau of the Mint at premium prices are excluded; however, uncirculated coin sets sold at face value plus handling charge are included.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [88, 700, 748, 789], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Average discount rate (PDO-2, -3)\\u2014In Treasury bill auctions, purchasers tender competitive bids on a discount rate basis. The average discount rate is the weighted, or adjusted, average of all bids accepted in the auction.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [88, 798, 751, 939], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Budget authority (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014Congress passes laws giving budget authority to Government entities, which gives the agencies the power to spend Federal funds. Congress can stipulate various criteria for the spending of these funds. For example, Congress can stipulate that a given agency must spend within a specific year, number of years, or any time in the future.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [86, 946, 751, 1102], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"The basic forms of budget authority are appropriations, authority to borrow, and contract authority. The period of time during which Congress makes funds available may be specified as 1-year, multiple-year, or no-year. The available amount may be classified as either definite or indefinite; a specific amount or an unspecified amount can be made available. Authority may also be classified as current or permanent. Permanent authority requires no current action by Congress.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [83, 1113, 746, 1156], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Budget deficit\\u2013The total, cumulative amount by which budget outlays (spending) exceed budget receipts (income).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [83, 1167, 751, 1214], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Capital (\\\"Federal Obligations\\\")\\u2014Assets, such as land, equipment, and financial reserves.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [90, 1225, 748, 1339], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Cash management bills (PDO-2)\\u2014Marketable Treasury bills of irregular maturity lengths, sold periodically to fund short-term cash needs of Treasury. Their sale, having higher minimum and multiple purchase requirements than those of other issues, is generally restricted to competitive bidders.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [90, 1346, 750, 1440], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Competitive tenders (\\\"Treasury Financing Operations\\\")\\u2014A bid to purchase a stated amount of one issue of Treasury securities at a specified yield or discount. The bid is accepted if it is within the range accepted in the auction. (See Noncompetitive tenders.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [91, 1446, 643, 1475], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Coupon issue\\u2013The issue of bonds or notes (public debt).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [90, 1482, 748, 1554], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Currency no longer issued (USCC)\\u2014Old and new series gold and silver certificates, Federal Reserve notes, national bank notes, and 1890 Series Treasury notes.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [88, 1563, 748, 1632], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Current income bonds (\\\"U.S. Savings Bonds and Notes\\\")\\u2014Bonds paying semiannual interest to holders. Interest is not included in accrued discount.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [90, 1639, 751, 1800], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Debt outstanding subject to limitation (FD-6)\\u2014The debt incurred by the Treasury subject to the statutory limit set by Congress. Until World War I, a specific amount of debt was authorized to each separate security issue. Beginning with the Second Liberty Loan Act of 1917, the nature of the limitation was modified until, in 1941, it developed into an overall limit on the outstanding Federal debt. In 1991, the debt limit was $4,145,000 million; the limit may change from year to year.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [91, 1806, 750, 1853], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"The debt subject to limitation includes most of Treasury's public debt except securities issued to the Federal Financing Bank, upon which\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [786, 414, 1443, 463], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"there is a limitation of $15 billion, and certain categories of older debt (totaling approximately $595 million as of February 1991).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [786, 469, 1443, 519], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Discount\\u2013The interest deducted in advance when purchasing notes or bonds. (See Accrued discount)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [786, 525, 1443, 619], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Discount rate (PDO-2)\\u2014The difference between par value and the actual purchase price paid, annualized over a 360-day year. Because this rate is less than the actual yield (coupon-equivalent rate), the yield should be used in any comparison with coupon issue securities.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 19, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [788, 626, 1420, 653], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Dollar coins (USCC)\\u2014Include standard silver and nonsilver coins.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 20, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [786, 662, 1445, 753], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Domestic series (FD-2)\\u2014Nonmarketable, interest and non-interest-bearing securities issued periodically by Treasury to the Resolution Funding Corporation (RFC) for investment of funds authorized under section 21B of the Federal Home Loan Bank Act (12 U.S.C. 1441b).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 21, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [786, 762, 1441, 834], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Federal intrafund transactions (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014In-trabudgetary transactions in which payments and receipts both occur within the same Federal fund group (Federal funds or trust funds).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 22, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [786, 843, 1443, 979], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Federal Reserve notes (USCC)\\u2014Issues by the U.S. Government to the public through the Federal Reserve banks and their member banks. They represent money owed by the Government to the public. Currently, the item \\\"Federal Reserve notes\\u2014amounts outstanding\\\" consists of new series issues. The Federal Reserve note is the only class of currency currently issued.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 23, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [786, 988, 1445, 1053], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Foreign (\\\"Foreign Currency Positions,\\\" IFS-2, -3)-(international) Locations other than those included under the definition of the United States. (See United States.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 24, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [786, 1064, 1445, 1243], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Foreigner (\\\"Capital Movements,\\\" IFS-2)-All institutions and individuals living outside the United States, including U.S. citizens living abroad, and branches, subsidiaries, and other affiliates abroad of U.S. banks and business concerns; central governments, central banks, and other official institutions of countries other than the United States, and international and regional organizations, wherever located. Also, refers to persons in the United States to the extent that they are known by reporting institutions to be acting for foreigners.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 25, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [786, 1250, 1443, 1453], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Foreign official Institutions (\\\"Capital Movements\\\")\\u2014Includes central governments of foreign countries, including all departments and agencies of national governments; central banks, exchange authorities, and all fiscal agents of foreign national governments that undertake activities similar to those of a treasury, central bank, or stabilization fund; diplomatic and consular establishments of foreign national governments; and any international or regional organization, including subordinate and affiliate agencies, created by treaty or convention between sovereign states.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 26, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [788, 1462, 1445, 1596], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Foreign public borrower (\\\"Capital Movements\\\")\\u2014Includes foreign official institutions, as defined above, the corporations and agencies of foreign central governments, including development banks and institutions, and other agencies that are majority-owned by the central government or its departments; and state provincial and local governments of foreign countries and their departments and agencies.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 27, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [784, 1605, 1445, 1762], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Foreign-targeted issue (PDO-1, 3)\\u2014Foreign-targeted issues were notes sold between October 1984 and February 1986 to foreign institutions, foreign branches of U.S. institutions, foreign central banks or monetary authorities, or to international organizations in which the United States held membership. Sold as companion issues, they could be converted to domestic (normal) Treasury notes with the same maturity and interest rates. Interest was paid annually.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 28, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [788, 1771, 1445, 1815], \"page_id\": 163}], \"content\": \"Fractional coins (USCC)\\u2014Coins minted In denominations of 50, 25, and 10 cents, and minor coins (5 cents and 1 cent).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 29, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 24, 96, 46], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"154\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [604, 203, 849, 261], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Glossary\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 287, 705, 528], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Government account series (FD-2)\\u2014Certain trust fund statutes require the Secretary of the Treasury to apply monies held by these funds toward the issuance of nonmarketable special securities. These securities are sold directly by Treasury to a specific Government agency, trust fund, or account. Their rate is based on an average of market yields on outstanding Treasury obligations, and they may be redeemed at the option of the holder. Roughly 80 percent of these are issued to five holders: the Federal old-age and survivors insurance trust fund; the civil service retirement and disability fund; the Federal hospital insurance trust fund; the military retirement fund; and the unemployment trust fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 543, 705, 625], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"International Monetary Fund (\\\"Exchange Stabilization Fund.\\\" IFS-1)\\u2014(IMF) Established by the United Nations, the IMF promotes international trade, stability of exchange, and monetary cooperation. Members are allowed to draw from the fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 636, 705, 705], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Interfund transactions (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")-Trans-actions in which payments are made from one fund group (either Federal funds or trust funds) to a receipt account in another group.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 722, 705, 806], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Intrabudgetary transactions (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014These occur when payment and receipt both occur within the budget, or when payment is made from off-budget Federal entities whose budget authority and outlays are excluded from the budget totals.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 822, 705, 906], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Majority-owned foreign partnerships (\\\"Foreign Currency Positions\\\")\\u2014Partnerships organized under the laws of a foreign country in which one or more U.S. nonbanking concerns or nonprofit institutions, directly or indirectly, owns more than 50 percent profit interest.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 923, 705, 1029], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Majority-owned foreign subsidiaries (\\\"Foreign Currency Positions\\\")\\u2014Foreign corporations in which one or more nonbanking business concerns or nonprofit institutions located in the United States, directly or indirectly, owns stock with more than 50 percent of the total combined voting power, or of the total value of all classes of stock.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 1043, 707, 1217], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Matured non-interest-bearing debt (SBN-1, -2, -3)-The value of outstanding savings bonds and notes that have reached final maturity and no longer earn interest. Includes all Series A-D, F, G, J, and K bonds. Series E bonds (issued between May 1941 and November 1965). Series EE (issued since January 1980), Series H (issued from June 1952 through December 1979), and savings notes issued between May 1967 and October 1970 have a final maturity of 30 years. Series HH bonds (issued since January 1980) mature after 20 years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 1232, 707, 1341], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Noncompetitive tenders (\\\"Treasury Financing Operations\\\")\\u2014Offers by an investor to purchase Treasury securities at the price equivalent to the weighted average discount rate or yield of accepted competitive tenders in a Treasury auction. Noncompetitive tenders are always accepted in full.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 1354, 707, 1398], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Obligation (\\\"Federal Obligations\\\")\\u2014An unpaid commitment to acquire goods or services.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 1411, 707, 1520], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Off-budget Federal entities (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014Federally owned and controlled entities whose transactions are excluded from the budget totals under provisions of law. Their receipts, outlays, and surplus or deficit are not included in budget receipts, outlays, or deficits. Their budget authority is not included in totals of the budget.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 1533, 707, 1597], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Own foreign offices (\\\"Capital Movements\\\")\\u2014Refers to U.S. reporting Institutions' parent organizations, branches and/or majority-owned subsidiaries located outside the United States.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 1612, 707, 1679], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Outlays (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")-(expenditures, net disbursements) Payments on obligations in the form of cash, checks, the issuance of bonds or notes, or the maturing of interest coupons.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 1690, 652, 1712], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Par value--The face value of bonds or notes, including interest\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [51, 1725, 708, 1813], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Quarterly financing (\\\"Treasury Financing Operations\\\")\\u2014Treasury has historically offered packages of several \\\"coupon\\\" security issues on the 15th of February, May, August, and November, or on the next working day. These issues currently consist of a 3-year note, a 10-year\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [753, 287, 1401, 351], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"note, and a 30-year bond. Treasury sometimes offers additional amounts of outstanding long-term notes or bonds, rather than selling new security issues. (See Reopening.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [753, 364, 1403, 431], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Receipts (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014Funds collected from selling land, capital, or services, as well as collections from the public (budget receipts), such as taxes, fines, duties, and fees.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [753, 446, 1403, 530], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Reopening (PDO-3, 4)\\u2014The offer for sale of additional amounts of outstanding issues, rather than an entirely new issue. A reopened issue will always have the same maturity date, CUSIP-number, and interest rate as the original issue.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [753, 546, 1403, 590], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Short-term (\\\"Foreign Currency Positions\\\")\\u2014Securities maturing in 1 year or less.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 19, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [753, 605, 1405, 689], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Special drawing rights (\\\"Exchange Stabilization Fund,\\\" IFS-1)-International assets created by IMF that serve to increase international liquidity and provide additional international reserves. SDRs may be purchased and sold among eligible holders through IMF. (See IMF.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 20, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [753, 702, 1405, 811], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"SDR allocations are the counterpart to SDRs issued by IMF based on members' quotas in IMF. Although shown in exchange stabilization fund (ESF) statements as liabilities, they must be redeemed by ESF only in the event of liquidation of, or U.S. withdrawal from, the SDR department of IMF or cancellation of SDRs.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 21, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [753, 824, 1406, 910], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"SDR certificates are issued to the Federal Reserve System against SDRs when SDRs are legalized as money. Proceeds of monetization are deposited into an ESF account at the Federal Reserve Bank of New York.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 22, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 925, 1405, 967], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Spot (\\\"Foreign Currency Positions\\\")\\u2014Due for receipt or delivery within 2 workdays.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 23, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 985, 1405, 1177], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"State and local government series (FD-2)-(SLUGS) Special non-marketable certificates, notes, and bonds offered to State and local governments as a means to invest proceeds from their own tax-exempt financing. Interest rates and maturities comply with IRS arbitrage provisions. SLUGS are offered in both time deposit and demand deposit forms. Time deposit certificates have maturities of up to 1 year. Notes mature in 1 to 10 years and bonds mature in more than 10 years. Demand deposit securities are 1-day certificates rolled over with a rate adjustment daily.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 24, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 1193, 1405, 1323], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Statutory debt limit (FD-6)\\u2014By Act of Congress there is a limit, either temporary or permanent, on the amount of public debt that may be outstanding. When this limit is reached, Treasury may not sell new debt issues until Congress increases or extends the limit. For a detailed listing of changes in the limit since 1941, see the Budget of the United States Government. (See Debt outstanding subject to limitation.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 25, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 1338, 1406, 1464], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"STRIPS (PDO-1, 3)-Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal Securities. Long-term notes and bonds may be divided into principal and interest-paying components, which may be transferred and sold in amounts as small as $1,000. STRIPS are sold at auction at a minimum par amount, varying for each issue. The amount is an arithmetic function of the issue's interest rate.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 26, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 1480, 1405, 1524], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Treasury bills--The shortest term Federal security (maturity dates normally varying from 3 to 12 months), they are sold at a discount.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 27, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 1537, 1405, 1604], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Trust fund transaction (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014An intra-budgetary transaction in which both payments and receipts occur within the same trust fund group.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 28, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 1617, 1405, 1681], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"United States\\u2014Includes the 50 States, District of Columbia, Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, American Samoa, Midway Island, Virgin Islands, Wake Island, and all other territories and possessions.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 29, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 1694, 1405, 1756], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"U.S. notes (USCC)\\u2014Legal tender notes of five different issues: 1862 ($5-$1,000 notes); 1862 ($1-$2 notes); 1863 ($5-$1,000 notes); 1863 ($1-$10,000 notes); and 1901 ($10 notes).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 30, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 1771, 1405, 1816], \"page_id\": 164}], \"content\": \"Worldwide (\\\"Foreign Currency Position\\\")\\u2014Sum of \\\"United States\\\" and \\\"foreign\\\" trade.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 31, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [483, 101, 940, 139], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"Treasury Bulletin User Survey\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [45, 174, 1380, 259], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"In order to assist the Financial Reports Branch in our efforts to revise and improve our publication, please take a few moments to answer the following questions. Your responses will enable us to enhance the quality of the bulletin in order to meet your needs better.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 340, 1380, 382], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"Name/organization, telephone number:\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 398, 1382, 420], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"Job function:\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [45, 440, 1146, 478], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"How often do you use the 'Treasury Bulletin'? [ ] Daily [ ] Weekly [ ] Monthly [ ] Quarterly [ ] Yearly\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [45, 494, 1053, 541], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"For what purpose? [ ] Investment purposes [ ] Report Preparation [ ] School/Work [ ] Economic Predictions [ ] Other\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 559, 920, 586], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"How valuable is the \\\"Treasury Bulletin\\\" to your work?\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 601, 1358, 820], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>What sections do you use?</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>[ ] Articles</td><td>[ ] U.S. Savings Bonds & Notas</td><td>[ ] Consolidated Financial Statements of the United States Government</td></tr><tr><td>[ ] Profile of the Economy</td><td>[ ] Ownership of Federal Securities</td><td>[ ] Statement of Liabilities/Other Financial Commitments</td></tr><tr><td>[ ] Federal Fiscal Operations</td><td>[ ] Market Yields</td><td>[ ] Trust Fund Reports</td></tr><tr><td>[ ] Federal Obligations</td><td>[ ] Federal Agencies' Financial Reports</td><td>[ ] U.S. Currency & Coin</td></tr><tr><td>[ ] Account of the U.S. Treasury</td><td>[ ] International Financial Statistics</td><td>[ ] Glossary</td></tr><tr><td>[ ] Federal Debt</td><td>[ ] Capital Movements</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>[ ] Treasury Financing Operations</td><td>[ ] Foreign Currency Positions</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>[ ] Public Debt Operations</td><td>[ ] Exchange Stabilization Fund</td><td></td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [41, 841, 897, 863], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"Is the information presented in a manner you can use/understand? [ ] Yes [ ] No\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [43, 881, 879, 921], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"Do you find the graphs helpful? [ ] Yes [ ] No\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [43, 934, 879, 1241], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"Would you like to see the \\\"Treasury Bulletin\\\" provide...? (check those that apply)\\nmore policy papers\\ninterviews with Treasury officials\\nmore data; explain\\nbetter presentation; explain\\neasier to understand tables\\ncomputer data access\\nmore graphs\\nexpanded sections; which ones?\\nother\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [45, 1357, 954, 1382], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"What other suggestions or comments do you have for improving the \\\"Treasury Bulletin\\\"?\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [398, 1782, 1012, 1811], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \"We appreciate your participation in this survey.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [401, 1811, 1032, 1864], \"page_id\": 165}], \"content\": \