d><td></td><td></td><td>&#10004;</td></tr><tr><td>Federal hospital insurance trust fund</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>&#10004;</td></tr><tr><td>Federal old-age and survivors insurance trust fund</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>&#10004;</td></tr><tr><td>Federal supplementary medical insurance trust fund</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>&#10004;</td></tr><tr><td>Harbor maintenance trust fund</td><td>&#10004;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Hazardous substance superfund</td><td>&#10004;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Highway trust fund</td><td>&#10004;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Inland waterways trust fund</td><td>&#10004;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Leaking underground storage tank trust fund</td><td>&#10004;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>National service life insurance fund</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>&#10004;</td></tr><tr><td>Nuclear waste fund</td><td>&#10004;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Railroad retirement account</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>&#10004;</td></tr><tr><td>Reforestation trust fund</td><td>&#10004;</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Unemployment trust fund</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>&#10004;</td></tr><tr><td>Investments of specified trust accounts</td><td>&#10004;</td><td></td><td></td><td>&#10004;</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [537, 769, 914, 808], \"page_id\": 15}], \"content\": \"TREASURY ISSUES\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [476, 181, 983, 716], \"page_id\": 15}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1423, 57, 1438, 82], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"3\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [396, 128, 1136, 163], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"The Need for Reform in the Financial Markets\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [650, 223, 886, 254], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"Nicholas F. Brady\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [95, 384, 756, 561], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"I'd like to talk to you about the need for fundamental reform in our financial markets, and about the role of the securities industry in helping to shape that reform. I believe that we need change to help assure a sound economy and to strengthen the worldwide competitive standing of our financial institutions.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [95, 594, 755, 850], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"Recent developments in the financial services markets clearly indicate that fundamental reforms are needed. The banks are contemplating additional writeoffs, especially in the commercial real estate sector. There are lingering concerns with Third World loans. Problems with LBO loans are evident as well. The media carry daily reports that banks are tightening their lending standards, even for good customers, and that the fund insuring bank deposits is under stress.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [95, 888, 756, 972], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"Internationally, the league tables tell us that we're down to one commercial bank in the world's top 25. Ten years ago we had four. Ten years before that we had eight.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [95, 1005, 756, 1089], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"In the securities industry, profits are way down and layoffs continue. Employment in the industry has already decreased by 20 percent--50,000 jobs--since 1987.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [95, 1124, 756, 1173], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"And the S&L cleanup presents a dark backdrop that adds to the developing gloom.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [95, 1213, 751, 1266], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"What should we make of all this? Are we taking too much counsel from our fears?\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [95, 1299, 751, 1438], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"I'd like to take a few minutes to put the current situation in commercial banking into perspective. I'm not going to paint a rosy scenario, but let's not look only at the negatives. Overall, the commercial banking system is healthy despite some pockets of difficulty.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [95, 1475, 755, 1760], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"U.S. banks have over $200 billion in equity capital and an additional $50 billion in reserves. And they raised a great deal of equity in the 1980s, despite problem loans in energy, agriculture, and the Third World, proving that they can build capital even in difficult times. In fact, virtually all of our major bank holding companies now meet the 1992 worldwide standards for bank capital, established by the Bank for International Settlements. By comparison, that is not the case for the banks of a number of our major international competitors.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [141, 1798, 756, 1818], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"Perhaps most important, the banks of 1990 are not the\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [791, 375, 1446, 488], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"S&Ls of the 1980s. As I've said before, they're as different as chalk and cheese. By a wide margin, the banks have more capital, are more profitable and better managed, and have less risky kinds of assets than the S&L industry.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [791, 521, 1448, 660], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"Our banking system's over $200 billion in equity represents about 6 percent of total assets. By comparison, the equity capital of the S&L industry was under $10 billion and less than 1 percent of assets in 1987, the year industry losses mushroomed.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [791, 698, 1448, 808], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"Finally, the Federal bank regulators--the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, the Federal Reserve, and the FDIC--are a highly respected group with a solid tradition of professionalism and concern for safety and soundness.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [784, 908, 1431, 1036], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"The legal and regulatory structure of our financial system is outmoded, burdensome, and inefficient.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [791, 1129, 1133, 1149], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"Cyclical and Structural Problems\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [791, 1186, 1448, 1297], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"So the current situation needs to be analyzed with balance, and also with the benefit of historical perspective. From that viewpoint, our current difficulties reflect a mixture of both cyclical and structural problems.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [791, 1332, 1448, 1650], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"Part of the problem now is the business cycle. Many economists say that we're now entering the down phase of the cycle. Commercial real estate markets are overbuilt, and we've had a sharp, temporary increase in the price of oil. Although some industries and regions remain strong, the economy is weakening. In commercial banking, the correction appears to be a particularly sharp one, in part because it follows a lengthy expansion during which the traditional lending standards applied by many banks clearly eroded. And in the securities industry, the current difficulties follow a period of unusually rapid expansion.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [791, 1743, 1450, 1824], \"page_id\": 17}], \"content\": \"These were remarks by the Secretary of the Treasury, November 30, 1990, before the annual convention of the Securities Industry Association.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 19, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [50, 65, 72, 89], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"4\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [596, 127, 850, 156], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"ECONOMIC POLICY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [52, 221, 703, 274], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"But we have weathered this kind of storm before, and we will weather this one as well.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [50, 305, 701, 511], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"However, there is an underlying structural problem that exacerbates these cyclical downturns. I'm referring to the legal and regulatory structure of our financial system. It is outmoded, burdensome, and inefficient. And its flaws are an unseen contributor to our financial institutions' current difficulties. We need fundamental structural reform, and, as Secretary of the Treasury, I am committed to this goal.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [50, 542, 701, 830], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"The last 20 years have completely revolutionized the financial services markets, bringing intense competition to banks and benefits to the consumer. Money market funds with credit card and check-writing privileges now compete directly with traditional bank checking accounts. At the same time, the banks' corporate customers have taken their best business to the securities and commercial paper markets. And individuals increasingly rely on credit extended directly to them by manufacturers and retailers, rather than by banks.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [50, 928, 689, 1099], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"Today, banks and securities firms sell the same products and services to many of the same customers, often with little regard for geographic boundaries.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [52, 1193, 706, 1454], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"The traditional role of banks is to act as a link between depositors and borrowers. Banks do the credit analysis for depositors who are not able to do it. Over recent decades, rating agencies have grown to perform this same credit analysis function. And the expertise of these rating agencies has made it possible for investment bankers to sell commercial paper, floating rate notes, and bonds, which reduced the importance of the traditional bank lending function.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [54, 1516, 450, 1541], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"Technology in Information Processing\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [54, 1577, 704, 1835], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"Many of these changes are a result of new technology in information processing. Technology has eaten away at the system of rigid segmentation and protection. It has made the financial services industry into one market. Today, banks and securities firms sell the same products and services to many of the same customers, often with little regard for geographic boundaries. Merrill Lynch competes with Citicorp. Salomon competes with Bankers Trust. And Morgan Stanley competes with J.P. Morgan.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [748, 216, 1399, 446], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"The geographic limits on domestic banking activities also conflict with the reality of the marketplace. Try to imagine an investment banking firm having to do business through separate corporate entities with separate boards in every State--Merrill Lynch of New Jersey . . . First Boston of California . . . Paine Webber of Florida. In today's world, the automatic teller machine and 800 number have rendered the restrictions on interstate activities obsolete.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [746, 479, 1397, 711], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"As our banks have faced ever greater competition in their traditional areas, they have had only limited ability to follow their customers as new markets developed. As a result, in an effort to maintain margins, they have found themselves choosing among the more risky and often less attractive kinds of lending--such as commercial real estate and loans to highly leveraged companies. The result is a system with too much risk and too little profit.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [746, 745, 1402, 945], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"Moreover, the deposit insurance \\\"safety net,\\\" by allowing banks to attract funds under Uncle Sam's guarantee, has slowed the pace of consolidation. In the United States, we have about 12,500 commercial banks, far more than any of our international competitors. For example, Japan has about 150; the United Kingdom 550; Canada 65; and Germany 900.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [748, 977, 1400, 1122], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"Many, including the SIA, have concluded that it's time to overhaul the system, to address these underlying structural flaws. There is now a developing consensus in Washington and in the markets--in fact, all over the world--that the time has come.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [750, 1155, 1402, 1358], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"As representatives of the securities industry and as direct competitors of the banks, you have a unique role to play in this debate. With that in mind, some may ask what the securities industry has to gain by cooperating with what some of you may see as an ongoing attempt by the banks to get into your business. Or, to put it another way: Why help a competitor?\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [750, 1389, 1402, 1534], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"Let me attempt some answers. First, I believe that it is clearly in the interests of the securities industry to gain the ability to offer a full range of financial products. That is what your customers are coming to expect. And that is what many of your foreign competitors already offer.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [748, 1568, 1399, 1742], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"Second, no nation can be a world class competitor without a world class banking system. Let's face it--the banking industry's profitability is an important part of the health of our financial markets and our whole economy. We need banks that are strong enough to stand by their customers in bad environments as well as good ones.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [750, 1768, 1404, 1824], \"page_id\": 18}], \"content\": \"And although their total share of lending is shrinking, banks are still the largest source of credit overall, particularly\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1425, 46, 1443, 68], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"5\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [640, 108, 897, 137], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"ECONOMIC POLICY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [96, 201, 750, 375], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"for small and midsized companies that do not have ready access to the securities markets. Banks also represent the primary clearance and settlement mechanism for payments. In short, without a healthy commercial banking system, we can't hope to compete in the 1990s, let alone prosper. A strong financial system is good for America.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [93, 402, 748, 669], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"To those of you who may face change with apprehension, I'd also point out that the securities industry has taken major regulatory changes in stride before. May 1, 1975-May Day--when fixed commissions on equities were abolished. I remember it well-the game was over; the securities industry had no future. Many firms considered resigning from the New York Stock Exchange. Of course, we were wrong. We changed with the times, and the industry went on to record profitability in the 1980s.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [95, 696, 751, 842], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"With the SIA's proposal for financial services reform, the securities industry has added its voice to those who seek productive change. The SIA deserves credit for acknowledging that the existing division of our financial markets no longer makes sense.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [98, 872, 755, 1016], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"The Treasury also understands the need for change, and, as you may be aware, we will come forward with a comprehensive proposal in January. I'd like to outline for you some of the principles that will form the foundation of our proposal for modernization of the financial services industry.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [96, 1078, 559, 1104], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"Modernizing the Financial Services Industry\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [96, 1135, 751, 1281], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"First, the administration feels strongly that issues of deposit insurance reform--that is, the extent and character of the safety net--are so closely intertwined with questions of reform of the industry's structure, that it makes no sense to treat them separately.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [98, 1310, 755, 1517], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"Reform of deposit insurance will be an important step toward assuring that banks operate safely and soundly. But the administration also believes that to be safe and sound the banking system must be profitable. For that reason, deposit insurance reform should only be considered as part of a package that also addresses the underlying structural problems of our system.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [93, 1544, 750, 1754], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"Second, reform must recognize the reality of the marketplace, which is that the financial services industry has become one market. We must eliminate outmoded barriers to the conduct of financial business that deny this reality and thereby limit the profitability of all financial firms-banks and securities firms alike. Securities firms and banks should be free to affiliate.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 1778, 751, 1811], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"We must also address the geographic restrictions on\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [789, 201, 1443, 404], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"interstate banking and branching. We are the only modern country that does not permit national banking. It is remarkable that, as the European Community approaches Community-wide financial services, we in the United States are still stuck on the question of whether to permit our banks and securities firms to operate nationwide in an efficient manner.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [788, 495, 1433, 627], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"Reform must recognize the reality of the marketplace, which is that the financial services industry has become one market.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [793, 716, 1450, 974], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"Third, and of signal importance, we've got to carry out this restructuring in a way that limits, rather than expands, the taxpayers' exposure. In the era of the S&L cleanup, that is the first hurdle that any reform proposal will have to clear. Newly authorized financial activities must take place outside the safety net, and without the benefit of subsidy. We will insist on strong provisions--firewalls, so called--to protect the insured institution and prevent it from subsidizing its affiliates.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [791, 1009, 1450, 1244], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"The securities industry has always conducted its business--in good times and bad--without the benefit of a Government safety net or subsidy. This is right and should not change. Subsidies and safety nets distort the allocation of resources and bring the deadening hand of excessive Government regulation. The industry gets paid well to take risks that Government-insured commercial banks should not take. You should want it that way.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [794, 1274, 1450, 1387], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"Fourth, it goes without saying that, when the Glass-Steagall barrier is lifted, banks and securities firms should find themselves on a level playing field, favoring neither side.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [794, 1420, 1450, 1564], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"Fifth, I do expect that the reforms we propose will, over time, greatly enhance the profitability, safety, and soundness of our banks. However, as mentioned earlier, the fund that insures deposits in commercial banks is under stress. There may be a need to shore up that fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [794, 1595, 1450, 1740], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"The fact is that the bank insurance fund is expected to decline to about $10 billion at the end of this year, and may well decline further in 1991. The fund needs to be strong enough to permit the FDIC to do its job effectively, and to warrant the confidence of depositors.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [791, 1771, 1450, 1831], \"page_id\": 19}], \"content\": \"The Treasury is evaluating a range of contingency plans that would shore up the bank insurance fund. Any such plan\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [52, 60, 80, 98], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"6\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [596, 123, 862, 156], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"ECONOMIC POLICY\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [50, 214, 716, 397], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"would draw only on banking industry resources to assure the soundness of the fund without imposing a burden on the taxpayer. Responsibility for the fund will be placed squarely on the shoulders of industry. In fact, responsible banking organizations are already coming forward to accept this burden, and I applaud them for doing so.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [52, 417, 714, 718], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"Next year's debate on financial services reform will surely be an intense one. As that debate approaches, I urge the bankers to stand by their traditional franchise of lending to sound projects and creditworthy customers. Their customers are the foundation blocks of their political strength and public support. It would be a mistake for banks to retire to the sidelines and invest only in short-term marketable securities. To do so would imperil not only our prospects for economic growth, but also the banks' standing with the American people.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [50, 743, 714, 921], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"And while we're on the subject of credit, I'd like to say publicly what I've said privately to the bank regulators before. Use some judgment. Apply some balance. In evaluating loans, let's not run a competition on pessimism. Don't overreact. Be mindful of the effect your behavior can have on the willingness of banks to take even the reasonable risk of\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [751, 216, 1407, 279], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"lending to good credits. We need a banking system that is a taker, not a shedder, of such risks.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [751, 330, 1089, 368], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"The Administration's Response\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [755, 390, 1410, 482], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"For our part in the administration, we intend to be a strong force for careful but fundamental reform, and for stability in the near term.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [750, 508, 1414, 687], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"While this may be a time of tough sledding for financial firms of all descriptions, we do have the opportunity to find new answers to some of the fundamental questions that have defined our financial landscape. This time, there is a real chance that some of the lines that were set down over 50 years ago may be successfully redrawn.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [753, 714, 1412, 923], \"page_id\": 20}], \"content\": \"Today, I want to leave you with the certainty that this administration is committed to promoting legislation that will allow financial organizations to reshape their activities to operate profitably and soundly in an environment made new by technological change. With your help, we can preserve and strengthen the American financial system as a worldwide leader.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1423, 49, 1436, 71], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [592, 104, 935, 128], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"TREASURY ISSUES INDEX\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [91, 208, 1436, 263], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Previous articles appearing in the \\\"Treasury Issues\\\" section of the Treasury Bulletin are listed below by title, issue, and page number.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [189, 384, 539, 424], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Domestic Finance\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [189, 510, 1164, 537], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"\\\"Issues in the Securities and Futures Markets.\\\" Glauber, Robert R. June 1990, pp. 3-6.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [206, 537, 1342, 616], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"A discussion on regulatory fragmentation and related issues in the securities and futures markets, stressing the importance of integrating the U.S. fragmented system so as to gain significant benefits in innovation, enforcement, coordinated market mechanisms, and globalization.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [186, 709, 524, 747], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Economic Policy\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [189, 835, 1342, 912], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"\\\"Direct Revenue Effects of Capital Gains Taxation: A Reconsideration of the Time-Series Evidence, The.\\\" Darby, Michael R., Robert Gillingham, and John S. Greenlees. June 1988, pp. 2-2.8.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [211, 914, 1342, 970], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"A report presenting results that indicate the time-series data, like the cross-section data, provide considerable evidence supporting the likelihood of direct revenue gains from reductions in capital gains tax rates.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [186, 1023, 940, 1051], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"\\\"Fiscal 1991 Budget, The.\\\" Brady, Nicholas F. March 1990, page 3.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [206, 1051, 1340, 1131], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"A statement by the Secretary of the Treasury on the elements of the family savings account, the capital gains tax reduction, and the home ownership initiative contained in the administration-proposed Savings and Economic Growth Act.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [186, 1186, 1338, 1241], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"\\\"Outlook for the Savings and Loan Industry after the Financial Institutions Reform, Recovery, and Enforcement Act of 1989.\\\" Glauber, Robert R. December 1989, pp. 4-6.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [206, 1241, 1338, 1297], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"A discussion of the savings and loan industry's future as it relates to provisions in the Financial Institutions Reform, Recovery, and Enforcement Act of 1989.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [186, 1352, 1050, 1378], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"\\\"Role of Saving in a Dynamic U.S. Economy, The.\\\" September 1990, pp. 3-6.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [211, 1378, 1338, 1431], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"A report on the declining U.S. savings rate and its negative impact on investment and productivity growth in the United States.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [189, 1489, 1338, 1542], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"\\\"Solution to the Savings and Loan Problem, The.\\\" Excerpted. Brady, Nicholas F. September 1989, page 3.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [202, 1542, 1338, 1597], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"Remarks by the Secretary of the Treasury on the administration's comprehensive reform plan proposed for the overhaul of the savings and loan industry.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [186, 1650, 1337, 1705], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"\\\"Some Economic Aspects of the U.S. Health Care System.\\\" Summary. Duggan, James E. December 1990, pp. 3-5.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [206, 1705, 1338, 1758], \"page_id\": 21}], \"content\": \"A report on evolving characteristics of health care and their implications for public sector finance and government regulation.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [65, 54, 82, 76], \"page_id\": 22}], \"content\": \"8\", \"description\":